1
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:21,780
RUTH VALENTINE: I was born in this section and lived here all my life. I was born in Fort Tidball. They called it Fort Tidball.

2
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:26,580
RUTH VALENTINE: It was off Logan Road, below the church.

3
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:30,700
RUTH VALENTINE: And the bricks were made at the same time

4
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:35,740
RUTH VALENTINE: as the house that Reverend Marshall lived in.

5
00:00:37,520 --> 00:00:39,560
RUTH VALENTINE: But what I thought,

6
00:00:40,100 --> 00:00:42,600
RUTH VALENTINE: Since this was supposed to be a Sunday school class,

7
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,400
RUTH VALENTINE: That perhaps you'd be interested

8
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:57,020
RUTH VALENTINE: suggested, in the cradle roll picture. Now, I don't think that is given anymore to members

9
00:00:57,020 --> 00:01:08,660
RUTH VALENTINE: of the cradle roll. Now, I was born in December, and this says cradle roll certificate, and

10
00:01:08,660 --> 00:01:14,320
RUTH VALENTINE: they brought young children to him, and he took them up in his arms, put his hands upon

11
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:15,580
RUTH VALENTINE: them and blessed them.

12
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:19,320
RUTH VALENTINE: This certifies that Ruth Elizabeth Douglas was enrolled

13
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:24,140
RUTH VALENTINE: as a member of the cradle roll of Bethel Sunday School

14
00:01:24,140 --> 00:01:30,620
RUTH VALENTINE: of Bethel Presbyterian on the 28th day of March, 1914,

15
00:01:32,240 --> 00:01:35,420
RUTH VALENTINE: Born on the 26th day of December, 1913,

16
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:38,660
RUTH VALENTINE: Signed by Mary Wycoff, Superintendent,

17
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:42,820
RUTH VALENTINE: And Henry Harley, or Mary Wycoff,

18
00:01:42,820 --> 00:01:48,600
RUTH VALENTINE: superintendent of the cradle roll and Henry Harley superintendent and it says

19
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:54,280
RUTH VALENTINE: Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it.

20
00:01:56,880 --> 00:02:10,120
RUTH VALENTINE: So then, at that time, I wasn't even baptized when they gave the cradle roll because my mother had written on the back, right here, baptized.

21
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:24,080
RUTH VALENTINE: Now I think I must have been one of the first that Reverend Reiter baptized. He was installed in May in 1914 and I was baptized in June 1914.

22
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:37,360
RUTH VALENTINE: Then, I was promoted from the cradle roll to the beginner's department, and that was September 1917.

23
00:02:37,360 --> 00:03:01,460
RUTH VALENTINE: In 1917, the beginner's department, I remember just mostly of the little wood chairs that we used, and Mrs. Reiter taught the class, and we met where the stairway up to the sanctuary is.

24
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:07,620
RUTH VALENTINE: They called the fire tire uh it was just a small little room and then there was steps

25
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:16,680
RUTH VALENTINE: up and there's a room above it uh this was dated 1917 signed by Mary Wycoff Henry Harley

26
00:03:16,680 --> 00:03:23,760
RUTH VALENTINE: and Murray C. Reiter and then I have a certificate of promotion

27
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:33,860
RUTH VALENTINE: of Bethel Presbyterian Sunday School. This certifies Ruth Douglas as qualified to

28
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:42,520
RUTH VALENTINE: be promoted from the primary to the junior department. And it was 1923. Elsie McConkie

29
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:45,160
RUTH VALENTINE: and Mr. Kravitz was superintendent.

30
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:52,300
RUTH VALENTINE: Then this was from the intermediate department

31
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:54,600
RUTH VALENTINE: to the senior department.

32
00:03:58,040 --> 00:04:09,680
RUTH VALENTINE: And Mrs. Hamilton S. Brown was Superintendent of the Intermediate Department, and H. E. Pellett was Superintendent. And this was in 1929 when I was in high school.

33
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:24,500
RUTH VALENTINE: But one of the things that mentioned Mrs. Brown, we had lightbearers, and it was mostly young people, and I think, I don't know how often we met.

34
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:34,380
RUTH VALENTINE: I can't remember much about what we accomplished, but I know one thing that was sponsored by the Lightbearers was the Tom Thumb Wedding.

35
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:46,120
RUTH VALENTINE: If you can imagine taking charge of about 30 youngsters under seven and getting them to perform.

36
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:52,260
RUTH VALENTINE: Now, I wasn't in the Tom Thumb wedding, Miller Maits back there was, and his sister was maid

37
00:04:52,260 --> 00:05:00,260
RUTH VALENTINE: of honor in the Tom Thumb wedding, and it was really well done, I think.

38
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:03,200
RUTH VALENTINE: I mean, that was the impression I had.

39
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:12,320
RUTH VALENTINE: I was one of the older ones, and wasn't a member of the cast, but I had help with the

40
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:13,520
RUTH VALENTINE: reception afterwards.

41
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:15,420
RUTH VALENTINE: And then another

42
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:17,120
RUTH VALENTINE: thing that

43
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:19,720
RUTH VALENTINE: I remember very distinctly

44
00:05:19,720 --> 00:05:21,020
RUTH VALENTINE: was the Christian Endeavor

45
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:22,260
RUTH VALENTINE: and

46
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:25,440
RUTH VALENTINE: I didn't attend regularly. I went

47
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:27,460
RUTH VALENTINE: once in a while and we had

48
00:05:27,460 --> 00:05:29,420
RUTH VALENTINE: picnics and so forth. I always

49
00:05:29,420 --> 00:05:30,880
RUTH VALENTINE: managed to get the picnics

50
00:05:30,880 --> 00:05:33,420
RUTH VALENTINE: but Sunday

51
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:35,400
RUTH VALENTINE: evening sometimes there was other

52
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:36,560
RUTH VALENTINE: things that need done

53
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:39,000
RUTH VALENTINE: and they had

54
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,400
RUTH VALENTINE: plays and I can

55
00:05:41,400 --> 00:05:47,640
RUTH VALENTINE: remember the one play in particular nothing but the truth. I imagine some of you are familiar with

56
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:54,800
RUTH VALENTINE: it but it is uh it was surprising to me how hard it was

57
00:05:55,310 --> 00:06:18,330
RUTH VALENTINE: To go 24 hours without saying anything that was not true. And that was what the theme of the thing was. And another one was Home Came Ted. And Isabel Hemlinger coached the plays and then they performed them in the Bethel High School.

58
00:06:23,050 --> 00:06:29,590
HOST: Thank you, Ruth. Again, we're open to any questions as each person speaks. Feel free

59
00:06:29,590 --> 00:06:35,790
HOST: to raise some questions. The next person is Miller Maits. And Miller, as I mentioned earlier,

60
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:41,730
HOST: Is a descendant of the Oliver Miller family who lived in the homestead down below. So

61
00:06:41,730 --> 00:06:47,270
HOST: he comes from a long, historic family. And he and his family have also been active and

62
00:06:47,270 --> 00:06:49,710
HOST: dedicated members of Bethel for many, many years.

63
00:06:58,390 --> 00:07:00,650
MILLER MAITS: Public speaking is a little out of my line.

64
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:00,970
MILLER MAITS: I know.

65
00:07:02,050 --> 00:07:03,150
MILLER MAITS: They're among friends.

66
00:07:03,890 --> 00:07:07,710
MILLER MAITS: My remarks aren't so much related to the church

67
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:09,010
MILLER MAITS: as they are to the place here.

68
00:07:09,770 --> 00:07:14,130
MILLER MAITS: I joined the church, I think, at the age of about 14.

69
00:07:15,030 --> 00:07:16,210
MILLER MAITS: I still remember.

70
00:07:17,990 --> 00:07:29,630
MILLER MAITS: As a small boy, here in the old place, a couple of my outstanding memories of being entertained

71
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:38,630
MILLER MAITS: Here at the old home, playing with a collection of old arrowheads, Indian relics and one thing

72
00:07:38,630 --> 00:07:48,130
MILLER MAITS: other turned up in the ground right in this area below us here every time they plowed apparently

73
00:07:48,130 --> 00:07:56,370
MILLER MAITS: was an old encampment when Indians were traveling back and forth. There's a spring there; it's still there.

74
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:15,780
MILLER MAITS: Another memory of childhood days here. As you come up from the circle, we cross a concrete bridge today. Years back was an old wooden span. I might have set in there five, six years old.

75
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:22,380
MILLER MAITS: old piece of string and a bent pin on it for a piece of hook sitting there fishing over

76
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:33,040
MILLER MAITS: Off that old bridge. In fact, I grew up around it. I've been up here all my life.

77
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:50,220
HOST: I'll tell you a little bit about some of the churches.

78
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:59,700
HOST: I think we've called on everybody who's here to ask to speak, and there are some other

79
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:03,880
HOST: people with historic backgrounds here that may want to step up and say a few

80
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:07,920
HOST: words and I hope you will. Let me tell you a little bit about our churches and I'll

81
00:09:07,920 --> 00:09:10,820
HOST: check our time to make sure we don't get into the church service. We still have

82
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:18,380
HOST: about 15 minutes. The first church after the Miller homestead, the church had

83
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:24,220
HOST: started there in 1779. The first church was built and there was a log structure

84
00:09:24,220 --> 00:09:27,880
HOST: which was built right across from our present cemetery

85
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:31,140
HOST: On the other side of the road,

86
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:33,240
HOST: Not the side of the road where our church building is now.

87
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:38,300
HOST: And that lasted until 1826 when it was torn down

88
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:41,160
HOST: and we replaced it with a brick structure for the first time.

89
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:47,120
HOST: And that was built on the present site of our church building.

90
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:51,440
HOST: In 1855, we built another church.

91
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:53,060
HOST: You can see here we were always building churches.

92
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:56,760
HOST: and every 40 or 50 or 100 years we build another church.

93
00:09:57,430 --> 00:10:04,730
HOST: That was built from some of the bricks of the Second Church. There's a kind of historical connection between the bricks of one church and the bricks of another.

94
00:10:05,290 --> 00:10:12,750
HOST: And, in fact, the bricks in our present church, which was built in 1910, some of those came from the Second Church and the Third Church.

95
00:10:13,170 --> 00:10:19,630
HOST: So, historically, we have even the bricks kind of carry through our history through the ages.

96
00:10:19,630 --> 00:10:29,890
HOST: It was kind of interesting that on Easter Sunday, a little bit prior to 1910, I'm trying

97
00:10:29,890 --> 00:10:40,970
HOST: to think of the exact date now, I think I have it here someplace, but 1909, yeah I guess

98
00:10:40,970 --> 00:10:48,570
HOST: it was 1909, Edna Croco, who was unable to be here this morning because she's not too

99
00:10:48,570 --> 00:10:54,790
HOST: well, was going to tell us this story, I understand, that she was in the church service

100
00:10:54,790 --> 00:11:01,110
HOST: at that time, and they were having Easter Sunday service, and just as the minister was

101
00:11:01,110 --> 00:11:05,950
HOST: getting up to preach the sermon, a tornado sprung up and took the roof off the church,

102
00:11:06,750 --> 00:11:12,930
HOST: Scared the people to death, naturally, and everybody rushed out and tried to find safety,

103
00:11:13,110 --> 00:11:15,570
HOST: But, of course, a tornado, there is no safety.

104
00:11:15,570 --> 00:11:28,630
HOST: Fortunately, the tornado passed and went on, but as a result of that part, at least, they decided to build our church finally, so we replaced that church. That was one of the rather exciting things that happened along the way.

105
00:11:29,290 --> 00:11:38,630
HOST: It's kind of interesting to know also that up through Mr. Yarnell's pastorate, which

106
00:11:38,630 --> 00:11:49,370
HOST: was up through 1960, I forget when he left, we had only had six pastors in that whole

107
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:50,050
HOST: period of time.

108
00:11:50,090 --> 00:11:55,570
HOST: That was for the first 175 years of our history, we had only six pastors in our church.

109
00:11:56,280 --> 00:12:08,440
HOST: And that was a rather unusual record. Each pastor would stay for 35 or 40 years. Today, that's not the custom in hardly any churches. Most pastors move on after six or seven years.

110
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:23,360
HOST: One of the other important bits of history, and I'm going to ask Jean to tell us a little bit about the women of the church. They have the most interesting story from the church, I think, of anybody.

111
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:37,280
HOST: In 1794, a pastor at that time was John Clark. He was an aged man at that time.

112
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:43,780
HOST: He was up in years. This was the time the Whiskey Rebellion had started. It started

113
00:12:43,780 --> 00:12:50,600
HOST: in this community, as you know, and there were about, I think about 500 men came from

114
00:12:50,600 --> 00:12:56,520
HOST: various areas around principally Canonsburg and even down into this area and they were going

115
00:12:56,520 --> 00:13:02,760
HOST: to burn down the house of General Neville who was leading the government forces at that time

116
00:13:02,760 --> 00:13:09,980
HOST: and was trying to enforce the whiskey tax laws and Pastor Clark tried to dissuade them from doing this

117
00:13:09,980 --> 00:13:15,460
HOST: pointing out that this was a new government that it was more important to obey the government even

118
00:13:15,460 --> 00:13:20,080
HOST: if the laws were at that point unjust that they could change the law through the legal processes

119
00:13:20,080 --> 00:13:22,460
HOST: and should not try to change it through force of arms.

120
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:25,100
HOST: He was unable to dissuade them, however.

121
00:13:25,280 --> 00:13:28,400
HOST: They attacked General Neville's house,

122
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:31,080
HOST: Which was over in Bower Hill, incidentally,

123
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:34,960
HOST: and his forces killed Major McFarlane,

124
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:38,760
HOST: Who was the major in charge of that group.

125
00:13:43,680 --> 00:13:47,240
HOST: Pastor McMillan, John McMillan, who founded our church,

126
00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:49,740
HOST: Never was a pastor of our church.

127
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:56,320
HOST: He preached and served at the Hill Church, which is out on Route 19, as you know, near the approach.

128
00:13:59,170 --> 00:14:17,570
HOST: Donaldson's Crossroads. He preached there from 1775. He started in 1775 and preached for 55 years, which is a lot into 1830. He also preached at another church near there called Pigeon Creek.

129
00:14:17,570 --> 00:14:26,250
HOST: Our church, as far as building is concerned, a couple of points that may be of interest to you.

130
00:14:26,810 --> 00:14:34,010
HOST: Up until 1947, I believe it was, we did not have a very large basement in our church, and we

131
00:14:34,010 --> 00:14:40,650
HOST: excavated one of the big projects we did was to excavate the basement at that time. Let me tell

132
00:14:40,650 --> 00:14:44,890
HOST: you a little bit about, I think I have some of the costs here. When our third church building was

133
00:14:44,890 --> 00:14:53,290
HOST: built in 1855, it cost us $6,000. We built the second church, or we built this church,

134
00:14:53,350 --> 00:15:00,710
HOST: The fourth church in 1910. It cost us $30,000 to build our present church. We built the

135
00:15:00,710 --> 00:15:08,650
HOST: parsonage across the way from that in 1874 for $3,200. When we excavated the basement,

136
00:15:08,690 --> 00:15:14,170
HOST: it cost us $55,000 to $60,000 just to excavate the basement of a church for which we paid

137
00:15:14,170 --> 00:15:23,750
HOST: $30,000 to build in 1910. We built an addition on our church in 1951, cost us $247,000, and the last

138
00:15:23,750 --> 00:15:30,150
HOST: addition, as I remember the figures, came out to about $400,000. So you can see how prices have

139
00:15:30,150 --> 00:15:36,090
HOST: escalated and costs of our church have escalated. Another little bit of interest, I think, is the

140
00:15:36,090 --> 00:15:42,870
HOST: oldest grave in the cemetery near our church dates back to 1784, the grave of Elizabeth Kitto, wife

141
00:15:42,870 --> 00:15:46,290
HOST: of James Kitto, one of the early Revolutionary soldiers.

142
00:15:47,150 --> 00:15:50,070
HOST: And I think I'm going to turn this over to Gene

143
00:15:50,070 --> 00:15:51,010
HOST: for a few minutes.

144
00:15:51,410 --> 00:15:54,290
HOST: In preface, I might say that in 1947,

145
00:15:55,550 --> 00:15:58,150
HOST: We elected our first woman deacon.

146
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:02,730
HOST: 1963, we elected our first woman elder.

147
00:16:03,150 --> 00:16:06,990
HOST: So we've been very, very conservative

148
00:16:06,990 --> 00:16:09,770
HOST: from the standpoint of using the talents of women

149
00:16:09,770 --> 00:16:10,410
HOST: in our church.

150
00:16:10,590 --> 00:16:12,210
HOST: Maybe conservative isn't the right word.

151
00:16:12,330 --> 00:16:13,610
HOST: Backward, perhaps, is a better word.

152
00:16:14,030 --> 00:16:15,430
HOST: Hidebound, maybe is even better.

153
00:16:15,830 --> 00:16:18,090
HOST: And so we just began to come to life

154
00:16:18,090 --> 00:16:20,470
HOST: in the last relatively few years.

155
00:16:22,170 --> 00:16:23,270
HOST: Another little point.

156
00:16:23,430 --> 00:16:24,710
HOST: I keep seeing something more.

157
00:16:24,710 --> 00:16:27,270
HOST: or Samuel Fife, who was a long-time elder of our church

158
00:16:27,270 --> 00:16:32,530
HOST: and goes clear back to and descending from John McMillan also, I believe,

159
00:16:33,150 --> 00:16:38,210
HOST: Is responsible, I'm told, for planting most of the trees in Bethel in the early days.

160
00:16:38,370 --> 00:16:40,310
HOST: He was a farmer.

161
00:16:40,510 --> 00:16:43,070
HOST: He had a great feeling about trees,

162
00:16:43,230 --> 00:16:47,570
HOST: And he had a lot to do with when we started to build homes and so forth

163
00:16:47,570 --> 00:16:50,250
HOST: of having trees built around those homes.

164
00:16:50,350 --> 00:16:52,150
HOST: That's something I have not been aware of.

165
00:16:52,150 --> 00:16:55,430
HOST: Jean, why don't you take over and tell us about some of the women's activities.

166
00:16:57,910 --> 00:17:01,370
JEAN SNOWDEN: It's very typical that men don't want the women to have a say.

167
00:17:05,470 --> 00:17:12,570
JEAN SNOWDEN: The present women's organization is the United Presbyterian Women, and all the women of

168
00:17:12,570 --> 00:17:15,290
JEAN SNOWDEN: the church are united in that.

169
00:17:15,870 --> 00:17:20,310
JEAN SNOWDEN: But through the years, there were three different organizations:

170
00:17:20,310 --> 00:17:29,650
JEAN SNOWDEN: the Ladies Aid Society. And one of the first accounts that I know of that the women

171
00:17:29,650 --> 00:17:43,470
JEAN SNOWDEN: were active was in 1785. They were called upon to help dress the seminary students. So they made

172
00:17:43,470 --> 00:17:50,090
JEAN SNOWDEN: summer clothes out of linen that they dyed with new mown hay. I don't know what

173
00:17:50,090 --> 00:17:54,550
JEAN SNOWDEN: color that would turn out to be. And then they sent their

174
00:17:58,970 --> 00:18:12,050
JEAN SNOWDEN: — I don't know what that means either. And then the material was sent back to them, and they actually made the suits for the seminary students.

175
00:18:14,110 --> 00:18:20,030
JEAN SNOWDEN: They got into many more activities and the women were always busy doing something.

176
00:18:20,030 --> 00:18:27,490
JEAN SNOWDEN: When the building that is the current building was built in 1910, the women had

177
00:18:27,490 --> 00:18:36,410
JEAN SNOWDEN: a lot of work to do. They wanted to make money for that project. So they put on chicken dinners.

178
00:18:36,970 --> 00:18:45,290
JEAN SNOWDEN: And one lady even donated 11 chickens, which was an enormous donation in that day.

179
00:18:45,290 --> 00:18:49,370
JEAN SNOWDEN: You know, Edna Croco told us at the UPW meeting a couple of weeks ago

180
00:18:49,370 --> 00:18:52,730
JEAN SNOWDEN: that they put on oyster suppers as well.

181
00:18:53,850 --> 00:18:54,750
JEAN SNOWDEN: So they were busy.

182
00:18:55,570 --> 00:18:58,750
JEAN SNOWDEN: Some of the things the Ladies' Aid Society did,

183
00:18:59,890 --> 00:19:02,430
JEAN SNOWDEN: They provided the church furniture,

184
00:19:03,550 --> 00:19:05,290
JEAN SNOWDEN: Helped with the church repairs,

185
00:19:06,630 --> 00:19:07,590
JEAN SNOWDEN: Helped with the decorating.

186
00:19:07,630 --> 00:19:10,950
JEAN SNOWDEN: They bought tablecloths and dishes and silverware

187
00:19:10,950 --> 00:19:14,990
JEAN SNOWDEN: and window blinds for the parsonage and hymn books.

188
00:19:15,290 --> 00:19:21,910
JEAN SNOWDEN: The Christian flag, the steam table, church linoleum and screens.

189
00:19:22,090 --> 00:19:24,210
JEAN SNOWDEN: They contributed to the organ fund.

190
00:19:26,150 --> 00:19:29,850
JEAN SNOWDEN: Does that sound familiar to today's work for the women?

191
00:19:30,050 --> 00:19:31,570
JEAN SNOWDEN: We're still doing it.

192
00:19:32,330 --> 00:19:34,050
JEAN SNOWDEN: So there was the Ladies' Aid Society.

193
00:19:35,050 --> 00:19:39,710
JEAN SNOWDEN: Then some of the women were more interested in missionary societies.

194
00:19:39,710 --> 00:19:54,430
JEAN SNOWDEN: We usually think of missionary societies being women's groups, but back in the beginning the first missionary society was organized in 1822.

195
00:19:55,230 --> 00:20:15,830
JEAN SNOWDEN: And of the 106 members, only 44 were ladies, and the men held all the offices. So they kept the requirement for membership was that you either donate 50 cents or goods equaling 50 cents.

196
00:20:15,830 --> 00:20:20,410
JEAN SNOWDEN: so they continued for quite a while

197
00:20:20,410 --> 00:20:24,730
JEAN SNOWDEN: and in 1840 the women decided that they had to have their own

198
00:20:24,730 --> 00:20:28,390
JEAN SNOWDEN: missionary society, they had their own officers

199
00:20:28,390 --> 00:20:31,370
JEAN SNOWDEN: except for the treasurer

200
00:20:32,190 --> 00:20:36,930
JEAN SNOWDEN: And the minister, Dr. Marshall said he had to take care

201
00:20:36,930 --> 00:20:37,430
JEAN SNOWDEN: of the money

202
00:20:38,830 --> 00:20:44,770
JEAN SNOWDEN: And then the other society

203
00:20:44,770 --> 00:20:53,190
JEAN SNOWDEN: was the Fannie Dietrich Society, which was started in 1887, and it was a group of young

204
00:20:53,190 --> 00:21:03,810
JEAN SNOWDEN: women who were interested in missionary work. During the years, much money was gathered,

205
00:21:04,050 --> 00:21:14,650
JEAN SNOWDEN: Worked for, and sent to missions. We are still carrying on that tradition. In 1945, the three

206
00:21:14,650 --> 00:21:22,830
JEAN SNOWDEN: groups were joined in one then to make our current UPW organization, and we are still

207
00:21:22,830 --> 00:21:30,550
JEAN SNOWDEN: carrying on the traditions of missionary work, the traditions of service to the church, and

208
00:21:30,550 --> 00:21:32,770
JEAN SNOWDEN: the traditions of service to outsiders.

209
00:21:36,370 --> 00:21:39,350
JEAN SNOWDEN: Dave and I are comparatively new members.

210
00:21:39,350 --> 00:21:46,890
JEAN SNOWDEN: We've only been members for 33 years, but we have seen the last two phases of the

211
00:21:46,890 --> 00:21:55,410
JEAN SNOWDEN: building since we came. When we came, the auditorium wing was not built, and the other

212
00:21:55,410 --> 00:21:56,910
JEAN SNOWDEN: wing with the chapel.

213
00:21:57,470 --> 00:22:27,030
JEAN SNOWDEN: The Learning Center and the offices and so forth weren't built, but the church building was crowded. The room which is now the choir room was the kindergarten room for four and five-year-olds, and in that tiny room there were as many as 70 kids each Sunday. So you know why we had to build the little children.

214
00:22:27,030 --> 00:22:32,670
JEAN SNOWDEN: met in the kitchen because there was no other place for them. When we came, our youngest

215
00:22:32,670 --> 00:22:38,710
JEAN SNOWDEN: was a year and a half, and that's where she ended up in the kitchen. So we have made progress.

216
00:22:39,610 --> 00:22:45,630
JEAN SNOWDEN: We can't sit or stand on our path, but we have to go forward.

217
00:22:49,730 --> 00:22:55,350
HOST: I see. I read the record of what the women did over the years. You wonder just

218
00:22:55,350 --> 00:23:00,010
HOST: just why they stood by so many years and had no offices in the church.

219
00:23:00,150 --> 00:23:06,890
HOST: But at that time, it was a practice not to give women offices of responsibility.

220
00:23:07,490 --> 00:23:13,090
HOST: And it's a terrific waste of talent and persons that you can see over the years.

221
00:23:14,370 --> 00:23:18,450
HOST: As Gene points out, and as we bring our little meeting to a close,

222
00:23:18,490 --> 00:23:21,070
HOST: we'd like to say that we've talked a lot about the past,

223
00:23:22,170 --> 00:23:26,090
HOST: But really, as far as Bethel is concerned, we want to look toward the future.

224
00:23:26,230 --> 00:23:31,490
HOST: We do have, we feel, a great future ahead of us with over 200 years now of history,

225
00:23:31,670 --> 00:23:35,090
HOST: Almost 206 or 207 years, I guess, this year.

226
00:23:36,290 --> 00:23:41,030
HOST: It is time for us then to turn our views outward and upward

227
00:23:41,530 --> 00:23:46,230
HOST: And think in terms of what Bethel can and should be doing in this day and age.

228
00:23:46,230 --> 00:23:55,550
HOST: And so we'd like to leave you on that note that we hope that you agree with us that Bethel has a great future.

229
00:23:56,150 --> 00:24:00,190
HOST: And that we are glad to be part of that at this time. Thanks.

230
00:24:03,570 --> 00:24:07,810
HOST: I don't know when the church service starts, John. Anybody have any feeling for that?

231
00:24:09,870 --> 00:24:12,090
UNIDENTIFIED: It's quarter to 11 now.

232
00:24:13,110 --> 00:24:13,570
UNIDENTIFIED: Yeah.

233
00:24:44,130 --> 00:24:52,230
UNIDENTIFIED: I'm glad you're feeling better.

234
00:25:11,970 --> 00:25:24,530
UNIDENTIFIED: I saw this guy. I was flat on my back.
